      "overview": "The lesson explains that some signs of the noun are physically observable in the word itself, such as the acceptance of al- (alif–lām of definiteness) at the beginning or tanwīn at the end, while others are not written but understood through meaning and function. The most significant semantic sign discussed is al-ḥadīthu ʿanhu or al-isnād ilayhi, meaning that a word can serve as the subject about which information is given. Through a detailed explanation of isnād (predication), students learn that a meaningful sentence in Arabic requires the attribution of information (musnad) to an entity (musnad ilayh), and that only nouns, by their nature, can function as true subjects of predication.\n\nThe lesson further clarifies why verbs (fiʿl) and particles (ḥarf) cannot serve as musnad ilayh when used according to their original grammatical function, since verbs inherently convey information about an action and its doer, and particles merely facilitate relationships between meanings. Apparent counterexamples—such as speaking about a verb or particle—are resolved by explaining that in such cases, the predication is actually directed toward the word as a linguistic entity (kalimah), which is itself an ism. Special attention is given to cases where other signs of the noun fail to appear, such as certain pronouns (e.g., the tāʾ in ḍarabtu), demonstrating why the semantic sign of being “spoken about” is considered the most beneficial (anfaʿ al-ʿalāmāt). The lesson concludes by emphasizing the practical purpose of studying these signs: enabling students to correctly identify nouns, verbs, and particles in Qurʾānic and classical texts, which is essential for assigning proper iʿrāb and understanding grammatical structure accurately.",
