      "overview": "The primary objective (gharaḍ) of Arabic grammar (ʿilm al-naḥw) is to enable the student to correctly understand and analyze the syntactic states of words, particularly the endings of words and the meanings those endings convey. Classical grammarians defined naḥw as the discipline that teaches the rules by which one understands the conditions (aḥwāl) of the ends of words—whether nouns (asmāʾ), verbs (afʿāl), or particles (ḥurūf)—from the perspectives of declension (iʿrāb) and indeclension (bināʾ).\nThis focus on word endings is not incidental. Meaning in Arabic is often distinguished not by word order alone, but by changes in the final letter or vowel. For this reason, the study of iʿrāb and bināʾ forms the backbone and essence of Arabic grammar. Any deficiency in understanding this topic leads to serious interpretive errors, especially in the Qurʾān, ḥadīth, and classical legal texts.",
