      "overview": "This lesson explores the different types of Iʿrāb (إِعْرَاب) in Arabic grammar, building upon a prior discussion regarding the purpose and theoretical foundation of Iʿrāb. Iʿrāb refers to the grammatical inflection that occurs at the end of words in order to indicate their syntactic function within a sentence. It is one of the defining characteristics of the Arabic language and plays a central role in distinguishing between ʿumdatu al-kalām (عُمْدَةُ الْكَلَام)—essential sentence elements—and faḍlatu al-kalām (فَضْلَةُ الْكَلَام)—supplementary elements.\nIn this lesson, attention is given to the two principal theoretical understandings of Iʿrāb—lafẓī (لَفْظِيّ) and maʿnawī (مَعْنَوِيّ)—as well as the practical categories of Iʿrāb: explicit (ẓāhir), estimated (taqdīrī), and local (maḥallī). Particular emphasis is placed on Iʿrāb Taqdīrī (إِعْرَاب تَقْدِيرِيّ)—estimated inflection—and the linguistic reasons behind it.",
