      "overview": "Having completed the discussion of Fiʿl al-Māḍī (فِعْلُ الْمَاضِي, Fiʿl al-Māḍī), the lesson introduces the second category of verbs in Arabic morphology: Fiʿl al-Amr (فِعْلُ الْأَمْرِ, Fiʿl al-Amr), the imperative verb. Rather than merely defining the imperative as a command, the lesson investigates its linguistic reality and explains why Arabic grammarians classify it as a verb despite the fact that a command appears to relate to an action that has not yet occurred.\nThe discussion combines concepts from Arabic grammar (naḥw), morphology (ṣarf), and rhetoric by examining the relationship between the wording of a command, the action being requested, and the grammatical concept of inshāʾ (إِنْشَاء), or initiation. Students are introduced to classical grammatical terminology while learning how scholars distinguish between the wording of a command and the actual occurrence of the action it demands.",
